{
  "tool": "brain_delegate_task",
  "args": {
    "persona": "devops",
    "intent": "Perform a detailed inventory and analysis of 100 AWS microservices for migration planning. For each service, use AWS APIs/CLI to extract the following information:\n1.  **Hosting Platform & Configuration**: Identify if the service is running on ECS, EKS, Lambda, or EC2/Fargate. Collect relevant configuration details such as Task Definitions (for ECS/Fargate), Deployment manifests (for EKS), Lambda function configurations, or EC2 instance details. Extract all environment variables, defined resource allocations (CPU, memory), and container/image definitions.\n2.  **Dependencies**: Discover all direct and indirect AWS service dependencies. This includes identifying linked S3 buckets, RDS databases, SQS queues, SNS topics, DynamoDB tables, API Gateways, Load Balancers, Secrets Manager entries, and Parameter Store values by inspecting IAM roles/policies, environment variables, security group rules, and service configurations.\n3.  **Inter-Service Communication**: Analyze and document how services interact with each other. This may involve identifying internal API calls, message queue integrations, event bus subscriptions, or other communication patterns.\n4.  **Resource Usage & Scaling**: Gather historical CloudWatch metrics for CPU utilization, memory utilization, network I/O, and concurrent executions over a representative period. Document existing auto-scaling policies (e.g., Auto Scaling Groups, ECS Service Auto Scaling) or Lambda concurrency limits.\n\nThe final output must be a structured report (e.g., JSON or YAML format), providing a comprehensive analysis for each of the 100 services to inform the migration design and strategy."
  }
}
```
